.TH TALLY 1 "August 27, 2026" "Tally" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tally \- count source files and lines, or inspect changes against Git
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tally
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B tally
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ]
.B \-\-diff
.RI [ revision ]
.br
.B tally
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\- \-
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tally counts regular text files and groups their lines by language.
The path defaults to the current directory; directories are scanned recursively.
An explicit file is counted on its own.
Language detection uses filenames, extensions, and selected content rules,
including shebangs.
Unrecognised text is counted as Unknown, with nonblank lines counted as code.
Files whose initial text sample contains NUL bytes or invalid UTF-8 are skipped.
.PP
A path of
.B \-
reads standard input as one file, not as a list of filenames.
Without a filename, language detection relies on a recognised shebang;
otherwise the text is Unknown.
Piping input without specifying this path does not select standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the installed version and check GitHub for a newer release.
This requires network access and can fail after printing the version.
When an update exists, print its release notes.
If both standard input and standard output are terminals, offer to replace
the executable; an empty answer accepts the update.
Otherwise, only print instructions for updating interactively.
The manual is not updated by this command.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Disable Git ignore rules during an ordinary directory scan, including global
Git ignores and repository excludes.
This does not disable all filtering: .ignore files still apply,
\&.git directories are still skipped, and explicit exclusions still apply.
It does not add untracked files to diff mode.
.TP
.BR \-j " " N ", " \-\-threads " " N
Set the directory worker count.
By default Tally adapts its directory scan, using up to twice the available
parallelism, capped at 8 workers; a single file uses one worker.
Values 0 and 1 select a serial directory scan.
Tracked-file, diff, and standard-input counting do not use this setting.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print extra diagnostics, including a breakdown of unknown file formats
on standard error after a normal file or directory count.
Diff and standard-input modes do not print that breakdown.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write structured JSON instead of a table. See OUTPUT.
.TP
.BR \-\-tracked
Count files listed in the Git index, using their current working-tree contents.
Requires Git and a working-tree repository.
Untracked files are excluded; staged additions are included.
Deleted files, nonregular files, and entries whose metadata cannot be read
are skipped.
Directory scope and explicit filters still apply, but ignore files do not.
An explicit untracked file produces an empty count.
.TP
.BR \-\-include " " glob
Include matching paths. May be repeated.
Quote globs to prevent shell expansion, for example
.BR "\-\-include '*.rs'" .
See FILTERING AND GIT.
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude " " glob
Exclude matching paths. May be repeated.
Exclusions are applied after inclusions, regardless of their command-line order.
.TP
.BR \-\-diff " [revision]"
Count added and deleted lines between a Git revision and the working tree.
The default revision is HEAD; an explicit revision may also use
.BR \-\-diff=revision .
An explicit directory path must precede this option:
.BR "tally src \-\-diff main" .
The next non-option argument after a bare
.B \-\-diff
is interpreted as its revision, not as the path.
See DIFF MODE.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing, useful for paths beginning with a dash
and for standard input.
.SH FILTERING AND GIT
Ordinary directory scans include hidden files and untracked files unless
ignored. Git ignore rules apply by default in repositories; .ignore files
are also respected. Directories named .git are always skipped.
Symlink entries are not counted or followed during directory traversal.
An explicit file bypasses directory ignore rules
but still uses explicit filters.
.PP
Include and exclude patterns use gitignore-style glob syntax.
Patterns are relative to the selected directory, or to the parent of an
explicit file. A pattern without a slash can match at any depth;
use a leading slash to anchor it to that root.
With positive include patterns, nonmatching files are excluded.
An include can override ignore-file rules, but explicit exclusions win.
Excluding a directory also excludes its descendants.
Use ordinary positive globs for these options; a leading exclamation mark
has negation semantics in the underlying matcher.
.PP
Tracked and diff modes require the git executable on PATH and operate within
the selected directory, not automatically over the entire repository.
They apply include/exclude filters but do not use directory ignore rules.
In diff mode, \-\-all and \-\-tracked do not change the selected files.
Standard input cannot be combined with \-\-tracked, \-\-diff,
\-\-include, or \-\-exclude; \-\-all has no effect on it.
.SH DIFF MODE
The path must be an existing directory inside a Git working tree.
The comparison is the selected revision against current working-tree contents,
including the combined effect of staged and unstaged changes.
It is not an index-only comparison or a comparison between two revisions.
Untracked files are not included; files added to the index can be included.
Renames are treated as deletions and additions.
.PP
Only changed lines are counted, classified using their surrounding file content
on the relevant side of the comparison.
Binary changes and changes without counted added or deleted lines do not
produce rows. Nonregular entries, including symlinks, are not counted as text.
Permission-denied errors are not generally downgraded to warnings in this mode.
.SH OUTPUT
Results go to standard output. Progress and diagnostics go to standard error.
Tables use colour when standard output is a terminal;
ordinary scans show progress when standard error is a terminal.
.PP
The normal table has Language, Files, Lines, Blank, Comment, and Code columns,
followed by a Total row. Languages are ordered by descending code count.
Lines are classified as blank, comment-only, or code; a line with both code
and a comment counts as code. A final line without a newline still counts.
Comment detection is language-based, not a full compiler parser.
.PP
Normal JSON has a languages array and a total object.
Each language entry has language, files, lines, blanks, comments, and code;
total has the same numeric fields without language.
Counts are integers, without the table's thousands separators.
.PP
Diff tables use the same columns with +added/\-deleted pairs.
Equal counts are abbreviated as \(+-N: for example, \(+-3 means three added
and three deleted, not a net change of three.
Files counts files contributing counted lines on each side; a modified file
can contribute to both counts. Rows are ordered by added plus deleted code.
Diff JSON has languages and total, with added and deleted objects containing
files, lines, blanks, comments, and code; each language entry also has language.
.SH ERRORS AND EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful count, help, or version/update operation.
Empty results and differences found are successful.
A broken output pipe is also treated as success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure, such as an invalid path, invalid glob,
invalid mode combination, Git failure, input/output failure,
or failed update check.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line parsing error, such as an unknown option
or invalid numeric argument.
.PP
During ordinary directory scans, permission-denied errors reported by the walker
or file reader warn on standard error and skip the inaccessible paths.
They do not alone cause failure; the result can therefore be incomplete even
with exit status 0. Other scan errors cause failure and suppress the summary.
Failure to read the initial path's metadata, or to open an explicit file,
remains fatal. Tracked mode can silently skip unavailable metadata, but a file
read failure is fatal. Diff mode can likewise skip unavailable working-tree
metadata; it does not offer a general permission-skipping guarantee.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tally
tally src
tally \-\-json src > counts.json
tally \-\-all .
tally \-\-tracked src
tally src \-\-include '*.rs' \-\-exclude 'generated/'
tally . \-\-diff
tally src \-\-diff main
tally \-\-diff
tally \-\-diff HEAD~1
tally src \-\-json \-\-diff main > changes.json
cat script.py | tally \-\- \-
.fi
.PP
The standard-input example is detected as Python only if it has a recognised
Python shebang; the original filename is not passed through the pipe.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR gitignore (5),
.BR man (1)
